Procedure and Queries
1. IN
Use IN to test membership against a list produced by a subquery.
 Students who belong to the 'Computer Science' or 'Mechanical' departments
SELECT name, dept_id
FROM Student
WHERE dept_id IN (
    SELECT dept_id FROM Department
    WHERE dept_name IN ('Computer Science', 'Mechanical')
);

 Students who are NOT enrolled in course_id 4 (DBMS)
SELECT name
FROM Student
WHERE roll_no NOT IN (
    SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4
);
2. ANY / SOME
ANY returns true if the comparison is true for at least one value returned by the subquery.
 Students who scored more marks than ANY (i.e. at least one) mark in course 2
SELECT DISTINCT s.name, e.marks
FROM Student s
JOIN Enrollment e ON s.roll_no = e.roll_no
WHERE e.marks > ANY (
    SELECT marks FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 2);

 Departments that offer a course with credits equal to ANY course's credits in dept 1
SELECT DISTINCT dept_id
FROM Course
WHERE credits = ANY (
    SELECT credits FROM Course WHERE dept_id = 1
);
3. ALL
ALL returns true only if the comparison is true for every value returned by the subquery.
 Students who scored more marks than ALL students in course 2
 (i.e. more than the maximum mark scored in course 2)
SELECT DISTINCT s.name, e.marks
FROM Student s
JOIN Enrollment e ON s.roll_no = e.roll_no
WHERE e.marks > ALL (
    SELECT marks FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 2
);

 Courses with credits greater than or equal to ALL courses in the Electronics department
SELECT course_name, credits
FROM Course
WHERE credits >= ALL (
    SELECT credits FROM Course
    WHERE dept_id = (SELECT dept_id FROM Department WHERE dept_name = 'Electronics')
);
4. EXISTS and NOT EXISTS
EXISTS checks only for the presence of rows in the subquery result, which can be more efficient than IN for correlated subqueries.
 Departments that have at least one student (EXISTS)
SELECT dept_name
FROM Department d
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM Student s WHERE s.dept_id = d.dept_id
);

 Students who are NOT enrolled in any course (NOT EXISTS)
SELECT name
FROM Student s
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM Enrollment e WHERE e.roll_no = s.roll_no
);

 Departments that offer NO courses at all (NOT EXISTS)
SELECT dept_name
FROM Department d
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM Course c WHERE c.dept_id = d.dept_id
);
5. UNION and UNION ALL
 UNION: all distinct roll numbers of students who scored above 80
 in DBMS (course 4) OR above 80 in Data Structures (course 1)
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4 AND marks > 80
UNION
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 1 AND marks > 80;

UNION ALL keeps duplicates (a student appearing in both result sets is listed twice)
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4 AND marks > 80
UNION ALL
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 1 AND marks > 80;
6. INTERSECT
 INTERSECT: roll numbers common to BOTH sets
students enrolled in course 1 AND also enrolled in course 4
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 1
INTERSECT
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4;

 Note: MySQL (< 8.0.31) has no INTERSECT keyword.
 Equivalent using IN / a join:
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 1
AND roll_no IN (SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4);
7. EXCEPT / MINUS (bonus)
EXCEPT / MINUS: roll numbers in course 1 but NOT in course 4
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 1
EXCEPT
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4;

MySQL equivalent (no EXCEPT/MINUS keyword):
SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 1
AND roll_no NOT IN (SELECT roll_no FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id = 4);
